<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch6-page2-repeated-games" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Repeated Games and the Wine Merchant</title>

  <p>
    Many games are not played in isolation. Back in Chapter 2, you played
    the Prisoner's Dilemma over and over against a robot that used the
    <q>tit for tat</q> strategy. Repeated games allow many new kinds of
    strategies, and many new Nash equilibria! For example, there is more
    incentive to cooperate in a repeated Prisoner's Dilemma: if you use a
    strategy that keeps cooperating as long as your opponent cooperates,
    then your opponent may be better off cooperating too.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 6.6 Discount factor and repeated games                       -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-6-6">
    <title>Section 6.6, Discount Factor and Repeated Games</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 6.6.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      The first sentence mentions Rubinstein's bargaining model from Section
      6.5, which we skipped (you don't need it). Then for the subsections:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            6.6.1: You can skim the salary argument. The important part is what the discount factor <m>\delta</m> means.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            6.6.2: This is the definition of a repeated game. We take a game
            in normal form <m>G</m>, called the <term>stage game</term>, and
            play it over and over at times <m>k = 0, 1, 2, \ldots</m> with
            discount factor <m>\delta</m>. This forms the repeated game
            <m>R</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul> 
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    The discount factor <m>\delta</m> is a number between 0 and 1. A payoff
    received <m>k</m> rounds from now counts as <m>\delta^k</m> times as
    much as the same payoff today. For example, if
    <m>\delta = \frac{1}{2}</m>, then in the first stage (<m>k = 0</m>) a
    player receives the full payoff of the outcome. In the next stage
    (<m>k = 1</m>), the payoff counts <m>\frac{1}{2}</m> as much; in stage
    <m>k = 2</m>, it counts <m>\frac{1}{2^2} = \frac{1}{4}</m> as much; and
    so on!
  </p>
  <p>
    To follow the computations in this section, you'll need the formula
    from calculus for the sum of a geometric series:
    <me>
     \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} r\delta^k = r + r\delta + r\delta^2 + \cdots = \frac{r}{1-\delta}.
    </me>
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-geometric-from-k">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In the computations in this section, we'll often need the total of a payoff of 1 per round,
        starting at round <m>k</m> instead of round 0. What is
        <m>\delta^k + \delta^{k+1} + \delta^{k+2} + \cdots</m>?
      </p>
      <p>
        Hint: factor out <m>\delta^k</m>, so
        <m>\delta^k + \delta^{k+1} + \delta^{k+2} + \cdots = \delta^k(\text{fill this part in!})</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>\dfrac{\delta^k}{1-\delta}</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Factor out <m>\delta^k</m> to get
            <m>\delta^k(1 + \delta + \delta^2 + \cdots)</m>, and use the
            geometric series formula on what's left.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>\dfrac{1}{1-\delta^k}</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Try factoring <m>\delta^k</m> out of every term
            first.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>\dfrac{1}{1-\delta} - \delta^k</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. The full sum from round 0 is
            <m>\frac{1}{1-\delta}</m>, but starting at round <m>k</m> drops
            <m>k</m> terms, not just one. Try factoring out
            <m>\delta^k</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 6.7 Wine Merchant, part 1: setup and the trigger strategy    -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-6-7-part1">
    <title>Section 6.7, The Wine Merchant and the Connoisseur (Part 1)</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 6.7 up to Theorem 6.1.</alert> Stop right before
      the statement of Theorem 6.1.
    </p>
    <p>
      This part sets up the game, shows that it is a Prisoner's Dilemma, and
      defines the <term>trigger strategy</term> <m>\sigma</m>.
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    Here is the stage game for reference, with <m>c</m> for cooperate and
    <m>d</m> for defect.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-wine-merchant-stage-game">
    <title>The Wine Merchant and the Connoisseur, payoffs (Merchant, Bernard)</title>
    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Bernard: <m>c</m> (pay)</cell>
        <cell>Bernard: <m>d</m> (steal)</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>Merchant: <m>c</m> (good wine)</cell>
        <cell><m>(1, 1)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(-4, 4)</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Merchant: <m>d</m> (bad wine)</cell>
        <cell><m>(4, -4)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    Before getting to the theorem, let's make sure the trigger strategy is
    clear. A player using the trigger strategy starts out willing to
    cooperate, but holds a grudge forever! They cooperate as long as the
    other player cooperates, but after a single defection from the other
    player, they never cooperate again.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-trigger-meaning">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose Bernard uses the trigger strategy. The Merchant cooperates
        (sells good wine) in rounds 0, 1, and 2, defects (sells bad wine) in
        round 3, and then cooperates again in every round after that. What
        does Bernard do in each round?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Bernard cooperates in rounds 0 through 3, defects in round 4,
            and then cooperates again from round 5 on, since the Merchant
            went back to cooperating.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. The trigger strategy never forgives. Once the
            Merchant defects, Bernard defects in every round after that,
            even though the Merchant goes back to selling good wine.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Bernard cooperates in rounds 0 through 3, and defects in every
            round from round 4 on.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Bernard cooperates in round 3, since he can't know yet
            that the Merchant is about to defect. He reacts starting in round
            4, and the grudge lasts forever, no matter what the Merchant
            does afterwards.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-trigger-vs-always-defect">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose the Merchant uses the trigger strategy, and Bernard always
        defects. What are the outcomes, written (Merchant, Bernard)?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>(d, d)</m> in every round, starting in round 0.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. The trigger strategy starts by cooperating, and it
            only switches to <m>d</m> the round <em>after</em> it sees a
            defection.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>(c, d)</m> in round 0, then <m>(d, d)</m> in every round after that.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. In round 0, the Merchant cooperates, since nobody has
            defected yet, and Bernard steals good wine, for a payoff of 4.
            From round 1 on, the Merchant sells bad wine and Bernard steals
            it, so Bernard gets 0. His total is
            <m>4 + 0 \cdot \delta + 0 \cdot \delta^2 + \cdots = 4</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Now let's think about when <em>both</em> players use the trigger
    strategy. Since neither one ever defects first, they both cooperate in
    every round, so each gets a payoff of 1 every round. Factoring in the
    discount factor <m>\delta</m>, each player's total payoff is
    <me>1 + \delta + \delta^2 + \cdots = \frac{1}{1-\delta}</me>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Compare that with the previous checkpoint, where the Merchant uses the
    trigger strategy and Bernard always defects. In round 0, the Merchant
    hasn't seen a defection yet, so he still cooperates, and Bernard gets 4
    for stealing good wine. From round 1 on, both defect, and Bernard gets 0
    every round. So Bernard's total payoff is
    <me>4 + 0 \cdot \delta + 0 \cdot \delta^2 + \cdots = 4</me>,
    no matter what <m>\delta</m> is.
  </p>
  <p>
    So if the Merchant uses the trigger strategy, should Bernard use the
    trigger strategy too, or always defect? Move the <m>\delta</m> slider in
    the widget below to compare the two payoffs.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-trigger-cooperate-or-defect">
    <caption>The Merchant uses the trigger strategy. Should Bernard use the trigger strategy too, or always defect?</caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-trigger-cooperate-or-defect"
      iframe="ch6-graph/trigger-cooperate-or-defect.html" width="100%" aspect="2:1">
      <static>
        <p>
          A slider for <m>\delta</m> and two tables of Bernard's discounted
          payoff in each round. If both use the trigger strategy, the outcome
          is <m>(c, c)</m> every round and Bernard gets <m>\delta^k</m> in
          round <m>k</m>, for a total of <m>\frac{1}{1-\delta}</m>. If
          Bernard always defects, the outcome is <m>(c, d)</m> in round 0
          and <m>(d, d)</m> after, so he gets 4 in round 0 and nothing
          after, for a total of 4.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <exercise xml:id="check-why-large-delta">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In the interactive, using the trigger strategy (and so cooperating
        every round) is better for Bernard when <m>\delta</m> is large, and
        always defecting is better when <m>\delta</m> is small. Why does this
        make sense?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            When <m>\delta</m> is small, later rounds count for very little,
            so the 4 that Bernard gets in round 0 by defecting matters more
            than all the 1s he would get later by cooperating.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. Defecting gives Bernard a big payoff now, and cooperating
            gives him a small payoff every round forever. The smaller
            <m>\delta</m> is, the less the future is worth to him, and the
            more the payoff now matters.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            When <m>\delta</m> is large, the Merchant is more forgiving, so
            Bernard is more likely to get away with defecting.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. The trigger strategy never forgives, no matter what
            <m>\delta</m> is. The discount factor doesn't change how the
            Merchant plays, only how much Bernard cares about future rounds.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    It doesn't take much more work to see why the cutoff is at
    <m>\delta = \frac{3}{4}</m>. Suppose the Merchant uses the trigger
    strategy. We showed above that if Bernard also uses the trigger
    strategy, his payoff is <m>\frac{1}{1-\delta}</m>, and if he always
    defects, his payoff is 4. So the trigger strategy gives Bernard at least
    as good a payoff as always defecting exactly when
    <me>
      \frac{1}{1-\delta} \geq 4, \quad \text{that is,} \quad \delta \geq \frac{3}{4}.
    </me>
  </p>
  <p>
    However, Bernard has infinitely many other strategies besides the
    trigger strategy and always defecting. For example, he could cooperate
    for the first 10 rounds and then always defect. It turns out that when
    <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{4}</m>, the trigger strategy isn't just better
    than always defecting, it is actually a best response to the Merchant's trigger
    strategy, so there is no strategy Bernard would rather use. Because the
    game is symmetric, the same argument shows that the trigger strategy is
    the Merchant's best response to Bernard's trigger strategy. That's
    exactly what it means for <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> to be a Nash
    equilibrium! The proof of Theorem 6.1 handles all of Bernard's
    strategies at once.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-6-7-theorem">
    <title>Section 6.7, Theorem 6.1</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Theorem 6.1 and its proof.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      The proof splits into two cases, depending on whether Bernard ever
      defects. The math gets more interesting in Case 2!
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Before reading the rest of Section 6.7, recall the last paragraph of
    Section 6.6.2, which defines a <term>subgame</term> of a repeated game. A subgame starts at some round <m>k</m>, and it
    <em>remembers</em> everything that happened before. So <q>the game
    starting at round 5, where both players always cooperated</q> and
    <q>the game starting at round 5, where Player 1 defected in round
    2</q> are different subgames. This is the same idea as on the previous
    page: a subgame is a <q>what if we got here?</q>, and <q>here</q>
    includes the history.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 6.7 Wine Merchant, part 2: subgame perfection                -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-6-7-part2">
    <title>Section 6.7, The Wine Merchant and the Connoisseur (Part 2)</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read the rest of Section 6.7.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      There are two ideas here. First, <m>\delta</m> can also be read as
      the probability that the game continues for another round. Second,
      the book asks whether <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is subgame perfect, and
      to answer it, the book has to redefine the trigger strategy more
      carefully.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Why bother redefining <m>\sigma</m>? Here's what goes wrong with the
    original definition, <q>use <m>d</m> forever if your
    <em>opponent</em> ever uses <m>d</m>.</q> Suppose that in round 0 the
    Merchant sells bad wine and Bernard pays for it. Look at the subgame
    starting in round 1.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        Bernard has never stolen, so the original <m>\sigma</m> tells the
        Merchant to keep selling good wine.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        The Merchant cheated, so the original <m>\sigma</m> tells Bernard to
        steal forever.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The Merchant gets <m>-4</m> every round, when switching to bad wine
    would give him 0. So the original <m>\sigma</m> is not a best response
    in that subgame. The redefined <m>\sigma</m> defects if <em>anyone</em>
    has defected, which fixes the problem.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-redefined-trigger">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Under the redefined trigger strategy, what does a player do in round
        <m>j</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use <m>c</m> if both players used <m>c</m> in every earlier
            round. Otherwise use <m>d</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use <m>c</m> if the opponent used <m>c</m> in every earlier
            round. Otherwise use <m>d</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite, that's the original definition. The example above
            shows why it fails in the subgame after the Merchant cheats.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Use whatever the opponent used in round <m>j-1</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Not quite, that's tit for tat.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    The book leaves the proof of subgame perfection to you (<q>you should
    be able to convince yourself...</q>). Let's do it. The key observation:
    there are only two kinds of subgames, depending on whether anyone has
    defected yet.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-trigger-spne-proof">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Put the blocks in order to prove that <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a
        subgame perfect Nash equilibrium when
        <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{4}</m>, using the redefined trigger strategy.
        Not every block belongs! Start with the case where nobody has
        defected.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <blocks>
      <block>
        <p>
          Let <m>H</m> be the subgame starting at round <m>k</m>, with some
          history of play in rounds <m>0, \ldots, k-1</m>. We must show that
          both players using <m>\sigma</m> is a Nash equilibrium of <m>H</m>.
        </p>
      </block>
      <block>
        <p>
          <alert>Case 1:</alert> in the history, both players used <m>c</m>
          in every round.
        </p>
      </block>
      <block>
        <p>
          Then <m>H</m> looks exactly like the original repeated game, with
          every payoff multiplied by <m>\delta^k</m>, and <m>\sigma</m> tells
          both players to play as if starting from scratch.
        </p>
      </block>
      <block>
        <p>
          Multiplying every payoff by <m>\delta^k</m> doesn't change which
          strategies are best, so by Theorem 6.1, neither player can improve
          when <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{4}</m>.
        </p>
      </block>
      <block>
        <p>
          <alert>Case 2:</alert> in the history, at least one player used
          <m>d</m> at least once.
        </p>
      </block>
      <block>
        <p>
          Then <m>\sigma</m> tells both players to use <m>d</m> in every
          round of <m>H</m>, no matter what happens in <m>H</m>.
        </p>
      </block>
      <block>
        <p>
          Suppose Player 2 uses <m>\sigma</m>. Then Player 2 uses <m>d</m>
          every round, whatever Player 1 does.
        </p>
      </block>
      <block>
        <p>
          Against <m>d</m>, Player 1 gets 0 from <m>d</m> and <m>-4</m> from
          <m>c</m>, and Player 1's choices can't change Player 2's future
          moves.
        </p>
      </block>
      <block>
        <p>
          So Player 1 can do no better than <m>d</m> every round, which is
          what <m>\sigma</m> says. By symmetry, the same holds for Player 2.
        </p>
      </block>
      <block>
        <p>
          Every subgame falls into Case 1 or Case 2, so
          <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a subgame perfect Nash equilibrium when
          <m>\delta \geq \frac{3}{4}</m>.
        </p>
      </block>
      <block correct="no">
        <p>
          <alert>Case 2:</alert> in the history, the opponent used <m>d</m>
          at least once.
        </p>
      </block>
      <block correct="no">
        <p>
          In Case 2, Player 1 should go back to <m>c</m>, since
          <m>(c, c)</m> pays 1, which is more than 0.
        </p>
      </block>
      <block correct="no">
        <p>
          By Theorem 6.1, <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is a Nash equilibrium of
          every subgame, so we are done.
        </p>
      </block>
    </blocks>
    <hint>
      <p>
        In Case 2, who controls what Player 2 does from now on? Can anything
        Player 1 does make Player 2 cooperate again?
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The correct order is: set up the subgame <m>H</m>; Case 1 (everyone
        cooperated so far), which is the original game scaled by
        <m>\delta^k</m>, so Theorem 6.1 applies; Case 2 (someone defected),
        where the opponent defects forever no matter what, so defecting
        every round is a best response; and the conclusion.
      </p>
      <p>
        The distractors: Case 2 has to be about <em>either</em> player
        defecting, not just the opponent, or the cases don't cover the
        subgame where you were the one who defected. Going back to
        <m>c</m> in Case 2 doesn't work, because Player 2 keeps playing
        <m>d</m>, so <m>c</m> pays <m>-4</m>, not 1. And Theorem 6.1 only
        covers subgames where nobody has defected yet, which is why we need
        the two cases at all.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Compare this to <term>tit for tat</term>: start with <m>c</m>, and
    after that, do whatever the other player did last round. On this week's
    homework, you'll show that two players using tit for tat is a Nash
    equilibrium for large enough <m>\delta</m>, but <em>not</em> a subgame
    perfect one. The difference is what happens after a defection: the
    trigger strategy's punishment is a best response, but with tit for tat,
    a single defection leads to an endless <m>c, d, c, d, \ldots</m> cycle
    of revenge.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Strategies for the homework, and optional robot              -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <p>
    The trigger strategy <m>\sigma</m> is just one strategy for the
    repeated game. Here are some others you'll meet on the homework. Each
    one is a rule that looks at what happened in earlier rounds and decides
    what to do now.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>Trigger, <m>\sigma</m>:</alert> start with <m>c</m>. If the
        other player ever defects, use <m>d</m> forever.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>Tit for tat, <m>\tau</m>:</alert> start with <m>c</m>. After
        that, do whatever the other player did last round.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>Suspicious tit for tat, <m>\nu</m>:</alert> start with
        <m>d</m>. After that, do whatever the other player did last round.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>Generous tit for tat, <m>\zeta</m>:</alert> play <m>c</m> in
        rounds 0 and 1. After that, do whatever the other player did last
        round.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>Always defect, <m>\beta</m>:</alert> use <m>d</m> every
        round.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <alert>Optional:</alert> if you'd like to get a feel for the strategies
    above, you can play the repeated game against a robot below. This isn't
    required, but it might help with the homework problem on these
    strategies, so feel free to come back to it then.
  </p>

  <p>
    Pick a robot and try to find your best response to it. The payoffs
    are the Wine Merchant's, with you as the Merchant, and
    <m>\delta = 0.8</m>. The <q>Mystery robot</q> picks one of the five
    strategies at random. Play a few rounds, and then guess which one it
    is.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-pd-robot-strategies">
    <caption>The repeated Prisoner's Dilemma, against a robot with a strategy you choose.</caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-pd-robot-strategies"
      iframe="ch6-graph/pd-robot-strategies.html" width="100%" aspect="4:3">
      <static>
        <p>
          Buttons to cooperate or defect each round against a robot using
          the trigger strategy, tit for tat, suspicious tit for tat,
          generous tit for tat, always defect, or a randomly chosen mystery
          strategy, with a running discounted total.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <exercise xml:id="reflect-robot-strategies">
    <statement>
      <p>Some things to try with the robot:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Against each robot, what's your best response? Is it ever
            better to defect?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If you cooperate every round, which robots look exactly the
            same? What moves could you make to tell them apart in mystery
            mode?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Against tit for tat, defect once and then just copy the robot's
            last move. What happens?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Against always defect, your best response is to always defect too.
        Against the trigger strategy and tit for tat, cooperating every
        round is best when <m>\delta = 0.8</m>, since <m>0.8 \geq \frac{3}{4}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you always cooperate, the trigger strategy, tit for tat, and
        generous tit for tat all cooperate every round too, so you can't
        tell them apart. Defecting once and then cooperating separates them:
        the trigger strategy never forgives you, but tit for tat does.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you defect once against tit for tat and then copy it, you get
        stuck alternating: you play <m>d, c, d, c, \ldots</m> and the robot
        plays <m>c, d, c, d, \ldots</m>. One defection turns into an endless
        cycle of revenge. That's the reason generous tit for tat exists.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-6-2" iframe="question-form.html?section=6.2" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
